.TH NEKTON 1 "2026-07-07" "nekton v0" "nekton manual"
.SH NAME
nekton \- signed statements (claims) about content-addressed things
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nekton
.I command
[\fIarguments\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nekton
records \fIsigned commitments\fR - claims an accountable identity makes ABOUT content:
a plankton foton, a file, a URI, or another claim. Where
.BR plankton (1)
records what is \fIreproducible\fR (checkable by re-running/hashing), nekton records what is
\fIattestable\fR (checkable only by \fIwhose\fR signature carries it): a description, a rationale,
a finding, a review, a tool validation.
.PP
A claim is a signed subject\(->predicate\(->object triple (RDF-flavoured, DSSE-signed). The kernel
treats predicates and context as \fBopaque terms\fR: it stores and indexes them but never
interprets vocabulary. It prescribes \fBno ontology and no templates\fR - those are federated
\fIdata\fR you bring. nekton depends on plankton (shared hashing/DSSE); never the reverse. It never
executes or reasons over the world.
.SH CONCEPTS
.TP
.B claim
subject (a hash/URI) + predicate (an opaque term IRI) + optional object/context + \fBby\fR (signer)
+ \fBwhen\fR + optional evidence - all DSSE-signed.
.TP
.B "alias / CURIE"
Authoring sugar in an aliases file, resolved to full IRIs BEFORE signing (so the wire form always
carries the full IRI). A \fBterm\fR is a short name (\fIreviewed\fR); a \fBCURIE\fR is a
\fIprefix:local\fR compact IRI (\fIgxp:reviewed\fR \(-> the prefix's namespace + \fIreviewed\fR); a
\fBtemplate alias\fR is a short name for a whole template.
.TP
.B template
A content-addressed JSON form definition (a predicate + typed fields). It is authoring sugar, not
the record: filling it produces one signed claim. Templates are DATA (read from a directory), never
built into the binary.
.TP
.B "scope / seed (subnekton)"
The one structural grammar the kernel enforces. A \fBseed\fR opens a scope (a bounded, federatable
sub-registry - a \fIsubnekton\fR); scoped claims chain under it via \fB--scope\fR/\fB--prev\fR. A
seed's id IS the scope id. This is how a set of claims (e.g. a shared template/ontology commons) is
delivered federated and vouched for wholesale.
.TP
.B nanopublication
The interop face: a claim RENDERS as a nanopublication (RDF/TriG) via
.BR "nekton export --nanopub" .
The DSSE claim stays the stored/signed form.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B nekton keygen \fIname\fR [--seed \fI64-hex\fR]
Generate an Ed25519 identity (\fIname\fR.key / \fIname\fR.pub). Same format as plankton.
\fB--seed\fR derives it deterministically so a corpus rebuilds to the same ids; fixtures only.
.TP
.B nekton pubkey \fIkey.key\fR|\fIhex\fR
Print the public key hex - what \fBverify\fR, \fB--trust-keys\fR and viewer key directories read.
.TP
.B nekton keyid \fIkey.pub\fR|\fIkey.key\fR|\fIhex\fR
Print the keyid shown as \fBby=key:\fR\fI<id>\fR.
.TP
.B nekton records [--json] [--since \fIN\fR]
Every claim together with its signed envelope: the SPEC Clause 12 \fBsync(since)\fR answer on stdout.
\fB--since\fR takes the cursor from the previous answer's \fBmax\fR.
.TP
.B nekton attach \fIsha256:id\fR --scheme \fIS\fR --file \fIF\fR [--media \fIM\fR]
Bind external verification material to a claim by its content address (SPEC 8.1): a Sigstore bundle,
a Rekor entry, an RFC 3161 token, an X.509 or eIDAS signature. It is stored beside the claim's own
subnekton, so handing a scope over hands its evidence over too. Stored, NEVER evaluated.
.TP
.B nekton material \fIsha256:id\fR [--json]
What verification material is attached to a record. Listed, not verified.
.TP
.B nekton nanopublish \fIclaim.dsse.json\fR [--rsa \fIkey.pem\fR] [--creator \fIIRI\fR] [-o \fIout\fR]
Render a claim to its nanopublication face and RSA-sign it, minting a Trusty URI - the external
publication projection (SPEC Clause 14). The projection's hash legitimately differs from the claim
id; the binding back is an explicit provenance reference, never assumed hash equality.
.TP
.B nekton annotate \fIsubject\fR|--foton \fIF\fR --template \fIname\fR|\fIalias\fR --set \fIk\fR=\fIv\fR ... --sign \fIkey\fR [--by \fIID\fR] [--scope \fIid\fR --prev \fIid\fR] [--templates-dir \fID\fR] [-o \fIout\fR]
Author + sign a claim from a template. The subject is a sha256:... , a URI, or \fB--foton \fIF\fR
(a foton envelope, whose \fIfoton id\fR becomes the subject - so the claim joins plankton's index
under the same id). Resolves term/CURIE/template aliases, hashes any file-typed field into evidence,
and auto-stamps \fBwhen\fR. Needs no jq/openssl.
.RS
.nf

  nekton annotate --foton fit.foton.json --template desc \\
      --set summary="mean is 1.5" --set rationale="central-tendency check" \\
      --by "CN=Analyst" --sign me.key -o desc.dsse.json
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B nekton templates [--show \fIname\fR]
List installed templates (from NEKTON_TEMPLATES) and their aliases. With \fB--show \fIname\fR (or a
positional name/alias) it prints that template's fields: name, type, required/optional, role, and
enum options - so you never have to open the template JSON to learn its `--set` keys.
.TP
.B nekton show \fIclaim.dsse.json\fR|\fIsha256:id\fR
Print one claim: subject, predicate, the object/statement, signer, and timestamp (from a file or a
registry id). The compact read that `about`/`by` (which return only pointers) do not give.
.TP
.B nekton seed \fIscope-name\fR --sign \fIkey\fR [--by \fIID\fR] [--parent \fIseedId\fR] [-o \fIout\fR]
Open a (sub)nekton scope. Prints the scope id (the seed's claim id). To fill the scope, on each
claim set:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
.B --scope
= this scope id - the SAME value for every claim in the scope (it never changes);
.IP \(bu 3
.B --prev
= the scope id for the FIRST claim, then the PREVIOUS claim's id for each next one (a hash chain).
.RE
.TP
.B nekton claim \fIspec.json\fR \fIkey.key\fR [\fIout.dsse\fR] [--add] [--registry \fID\fR]
Author + sign a claim from an explicit JSON spec (when a template does not fit). The authoring spec
differs from the wire claim: \fBsubject\fR is an ARRAY and \fBpredicate\fR is a BARE string.
.RS
.nf

  {
    "subject":   [ {"hash":"sha256:…"} | {"uri":"…"} ],           # REQUIRED (array)
    "predicate": "<IRI or alias>",                                # REQUIRED (string)
    "object":    {"value":"…"} | {"hash":"sha256:…"} | {"uri":"…"},  # optional
    "by":        "<identity>",  "when": "<RFC3339 UTC>",          # REQUIRED
    "why":       "…",  "evidence": [ {"hash":"sha256:…"} ],       # optional
    "scope":     "sha256:<seedId>",  "prev": "sha256:<prevId>"    # for a SCOPED / chained claim
  }
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B nekton add \fIenvelope.dsse.json\fR [--registry \fID\fR]
Ingest a signed claim (enforces the scope chain) into the registry (default ./nekton-data).
.TP
.B nekton verify \fIenvelope.dsse.json\fR \fIpubkey.pub\fR|\fIhex\fR
Verify a DSSE signature and report the claim (prints the \fBclaim id\fR). The public key may be a
\fB.pub file path\fR OR the hex string itself.
.TP
.B nekton about \fIsubject\fR
Claims about a subject (a "sha256:..." hash or a URI).
.TP
.B nekton by \fIsigner\fR|\fIpredicate\fR|\fIobject\fR \fIvalue\fR
Claims by signer, predicate term, or object. For \fBsigner\fR the value may be a keyid, a .pub file,
or a public-key hex - all are normalised to the keyid.
.TP
.B nekton head \fIscope-id\fR
Print the tip of a scope's hash chain: the in-scope claim that no other names as \fBprev\fR. Because
each claim id covers its \fBprev\fR, the head transitively commits to the whole chain, so publishing
it (or \fBkton anchor\fR on it) makes every prior edit in the scope tamper-evident. A linear chain has
one head; a branched scope (claims sharing a \fBprev\fR) has several. An empty scope reports the seed
itself as its tip.
.TP
.B nekton export [--title \fIT\fR] [\fIout\fR]
Serialize claims as JSON.
.TP
.B nekton export --nanopub \fIclaim.dsse.json\fR|\fIsha256:id\fR [-o \fIout\fR]
Render a claim (or a seed) to its nanopublication (RDF/TriG) face.
.TP
.B nekton mirror \fIlocal-registry-dir\fR
Overlay a peer's claims by hash (local, no network) - how a session receives a subnekton
commons. For a URL peer use
.BR "kton mirror" .
To author a scoped/chained claim you must first mirror the registry holding its scope seed and every
\fBprev\fR ancestor: \fBadd\fR refuses a claim whose \fBprev\fR does not resolve locally.
.TP
.B nekton man
Print this manual page (roff source; pipe to \fBman -l -\fR to render). Embedded in the binary.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NEKTON_DIR
Registry directory (default ./nekton-data).
.TP
.B NEKTON_TEMPLATES
Template directory (default ./templates) - federated data, not built in.
.TP
.B NEKTON_ALIASES
Alias file (default ./aliases.json) - CURIE/term/template sugar.
.SH EXAMPLES
Receive a shared commons (templates + ontology, delivered as a seeded subnekton), then use it:
.RS
.nf

  nekton mirror ../lab/commons/nekton-data     # federated delivery, no server
  export NEKTON_TEMPLATES=../lab/share/templates NEKTON_ALIASES=../lab/share/aliases.json
  nekton templates                             # see what's available (with aliases)
  nekton annotate --foton r.foton.json --template result \\
      --set statement="early intervention lowers the peak more" --set holds=supported \\
      --by "CN=Me" --sign me.key -o finding.dsse.json
  nekton add finding.dsse.json
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR plankton (1),
.BR kton (1)
